{"lines":[{"h":0.011778563015312105,"c":1,"t":"Test 3","y":0.10895170805124277,"w":0.053497943878173859,"x":0.21193415552593364},{"w":0.72863042195638028,"c":1,"h":0.018160007867712102,"y":0.14874410359860213,"t":"E Finally, understanding implicit theories of intelligence can help elucidate developmental and","x":0.21180744095298745},{"y":0.16505922292009167,"t":"cross-cultural differences. As mentioned earlier, people have expectations for intellectual","x":0.250915657134755,"w":0.66277374267578115,"h":0.017639637555053822,"c":1},{"t":"performances that differ for children of different ages. How these expectations differ is","c":1,"w":0.64431233723958325,"y":0.17986019054712266,"h":0.018201972907507802,"x":0.25089975263945508},{"t":"in part a function of culture. For example, expectations for children who participate in","y":0.195271630093107,"c":1,"x":0.25089811208835083,"w":0.6422410074869791,"h":0.017511903607803192},{"w":0.66068923950195302,"x":0.2529815671090459,"y":0.21181765770161975,"c":1,"t":"Western-style schooling are almost certain to be different from those for children who do","h":0.015553108233304691},{"c":1,"t":"not participate in such schooling.","x":0.24880788220470862,"y":0.22589302941296485,"h":0.016986184181959296,"w":0.25344390869140621},{"h":0.016860199199267623,"y":0.25074761776702803,"c":1,"x":0.24885645915145271,"t":"I have suggested that there are three major implicit theories of how intelligence relates to","w":0.66482930501302084},{"h":0.017069183053060488,"c":1,"y":0.26649970999120409,"t":"society as a whole (Sternberg, 1997). These might be called Hamiltonian, Jeffersonian, and","w":0.67711430867513012,"x":0.2509415323043292},{"x":0.24886721925598426,"w":0.66069580078125001,"t":"Jacksonian. These views are not based strictly, but rather, loosely, on the philosophies of","h":0.017144908612973575,"c":1,"y":0.2817099232264767},{"w":0.66901560465494792,"y":0.29637079795493781,"h":0.018739456563169998,"x":0.2508761698743831,"t":"Alexander Hamilton, Thomas Jefferson, and Andrew Jackson, three great statesmen in the","c":1},{"y":0.31242638384543198,"w":0.2119341532389323,"x":0.24897119469683474,"c":1,"t":"history of the United States.","h":0.013250883392226132},{"c":1,"y":0.33492987194581569,"h":0.020868332843758108,"t":"G The Hamiltonian view, which is similar to the Platonic view, is that people are born with","x":0.2076194750240512,"w":0.69996093749999999},{"t":"different levels of intelligence and that those who are less intelligent need the good offices","x":0.24680219862220554,"w":0.67305948893229162,"y":0.35160872562238299,"h":0.019466643900697189,"c":1},{"c":1,"y":0.36756748037980791,"t":"of the more intelligent to keep them in line, whether they are called government officials or,","x":0.2488837498699138,"w":0.68333740234374996,"h":0.016989533836625381},{"w":0.67514246622721352,"t":"in Plato's term, philosopher-kings. Herrnstein and Murray (1994) seem to have shared this","y":0.38266648310997287,"x":0.24678364924964058,"h":0.018211198498980785,"c":1},{"c":1,"y":0.39764342566010424,"t":"belief when they wrote about the emergence of a cognitive (high-IQ) elite, which eventually","x":0.24676983207445308,"w":0.68547632853190099,"h":0.019807826784670857},{"t":"would have to take responsibility for the largely irresponsible masses of non-elite (low-IQ)","c":1,"h":0.017939144646742289,"x":0.24886145604593035,"y":0.41396143518414141,"w":0.67513102213541654},{"c":1,"t":"people who cannot take care of themselves. Left to themselves, the unintelligent would","y":0.4285504573981026,"w":0.64837336222330721,"h":0.018700170573132335,"x":0.24885410792019078},{"y":0.44611307392542243,"w":0.38683128356933594,"t":"create, as they always have created, a kind of chaos.","h":0.013250883392226132,"x":0.24897118939722504,"c":1},{"t":"The Jeffersonian view is that people should have equal opportunities, but they do not","y":0.46762401470977355,"x":0.24675219849218707,"h":0.019576606256240026,"c":1,"w":0.63404968261718742},{"h":0.017597953338083894,"y":0.48335226396266107,"w":0.66894755045572918,"c":1,"x":0.246816201230208,"t":"necessarily avail themselves equally of these opportunities and are not necessarily equally"},{"w":0.68749582926432296,"h":0.018313283212614606,"c":1,"t":"rewarded for their accomplishments. People are rewarded for what they accomplish, if given","x":0.2467892748849882,"y":0.49866578248089222},{"x":0.24684461882599976,"c":1,"t":"equal opportunity. Low achievers are not rewarded to the same extent as high achievers.","w":0.65448104858398437,"h":0.015423032223686794,"y":0.5154525773551013},{"h":0.017185064030479924,"c":1,"x":0.246788886251533,"y":0.53013860744662111,"w":0.64837793986002601,"t":"In the Jeffersonian view, the goal of education is not to favor or foster an elite, as in the"},{"w":0.67518859863281244,"x":0.24676483266556923,"h":0.018502219686519439,"y":0.54473869359993299,"t":"Hamiltonian tradition, but rather to allow children the opportunities to make full use of the","c":1},{"h":0.018246566448953061,"t":"skills they have. My own views are similar to these (Sternberg, 1997).","w":0.52291305541992195,"c":1,"x":0.24472191759860779,"y":0.56085662221768018},{"h":0.018226250602443539,"y":0.58508689973745731,"w":0.65041559855143238,"x":0.24679335623124946,"c":1,"t":"The Jacksonian view is that all people are equal, not only as human beings but in terms"},{"y":0.60011219628689416,"h":0.017131538076591735,"x":0.24679484041336849,"t":"of their competencies - that one person would serve as well as another in government or","w":0.65864771525065091,"c":1},{"t":"on a jury or in almost any position of responsibility. In this view of democracy, people are","y":0.6156075864065198,"x":0.2447330333080121,"w":0.6710294087727865,"h":0.018630679279951989,"c":1},{"x":0.24471730238314118,"h":0.020195856532443934,"t":"essentially intersubstitutable except for specialized skills, all of which can be learned. In","y":0.63025578165317953,"w":0.65872924804687505,"c":1},{"y":0.64589055856281907,"t":"this view, we do not need or want any institutions that might lead to favoring one group","x":0.24467457984974525,"h":0.019935075470078556,"w":0.65055923461914056,"c":1},{"y":0.66289610088955986,"w":0.099342498779296878,"h":0.011730032337849194,"c":1,"t":"over another.","x":0.24666876333277268},{"h":0.020206916458615165,"x":0.24263679816590819,"y":0.68503084976812134,"c":1,"w":0.6608068847656251,"t":"Implicit theories of intelligence and of the relationship of intelligence to society perhaps"},{"x":0.24679020312177641,"y":0.70104933239470146,"t":"need to be considered more carefully than they have been because they often serve as","w":0.6339934794108073,"c":1,"h":0.017807817852258379},{"w":0.67921691894531255,"h":0.016856469872141067,"c":1,"t":"underlying presuppositions for explicit theories and even experimental designs that are then","x":0.24681198734918591,"y":0.71678696049933688},{"y":0.73086457682545758,"h":0.018163220480839071,"w":0.65458501180013018,"c":1,"t":"taken as scientific contributions. Until scholars are able to discuss their implicit theories","x":0.24474552125852653},{"h":0.019038464351874285,"t":"and thus their assumptions, they are likely to miss the point of what others are saying when","y":0.7477338660627475,"c":1,"x":0.24268509212476128,"w":0.67923909505208335},{"w":0.3539094543457032,"c":1,"h":0.014723203769140158,"t":"discussing their explicit theories and their data.","x":0.24279835912872774,"y":0.76590106020943771},{"x":0.20164609062878672,"y":0.86482558121833408,"w":0.024691357612609877,"t":"60","h":0.013081395583944722,"c":1}],"w":2400,"h":3395}